/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (U-Net is a CNN architecture popular for image segmentation, and is perfect for medical images, because it works well with small datasets and locates even low-contrast structures.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (It does not produce a class, but produces an output map, i.e., a pixel-wise mask. In fact, for each pixel of the image, U-Net decides: lesion or background.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (There are two main components: )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 566 0 R
/E (The Encoder \(contracting path\): progressively extracts high-level features from the input image and reduces spatial resolution.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
291 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 567 0 R
/E (The Decoder \(expanding path\): reconstructs the segmentation map by upsampling feature maps and progressively restores the original image resolution, to produce pixel-wise predictions.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
292 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (A key feature of U-Net is the presence of Skip Connections, which directly connect encoder layers to their corresponding decoder layers, transfering high-resolution spatial information from the encoder to the decoder. They help to preserve object boundaries and improve localization accuracy.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
